// fleet domain -- writes.
//
// THE OWNERSHIP RULE, stated once and obeyed everywhere below: `ownerUserId` is
// stamped from `actor.userId`, never accepted from a caller argument, on every
// insert AND on every update. Never from an argument, because a caller who can
// name the owner can create rows owned by somebody else. On updates too,
// because an update that omits it leaves the field to the read-merge, and a row
// whose ownership depends on a merge is a row whose ownership can drift.
//
// THE THREE WRITERS. Who calls what matters more here than in most domains,
// because some of these rows decide what infrastructure exists and what money
// is owed:
//
//   1. THE CUSTOMER, through Orbit. `createSubscriber`, `updateSubscriberBilling`,
//      `requestTierChange`, `setSubscriptionAddOns`, `requestCancellation`.
//      These are ordinary owned-tier writes and they state INTENT: they move a
//      row the automations then act on. Note what is NOT here -- there is no
//      customer-callable mutation that sets a subscription to `active`, moves a
//      period boundary, or writes a Stripe id. A customer cannot pay themselves.
//
//   2. THE STRIPE WEBHOOK PATH, through the inbound receiver's automations
//      (task memql#3854). `activateSubscription`, `recordPaymentFailure`,
//      `recordPaymentRecovery`, `setSubscriptionPeriod`, `linkStripeCustomer`,
//      `linkStripeSubscription`. Every one of these is reached only from an
//      automation triggered by a SIGNATURE-VERIFIED v1:platform:inboundRequest
//      row. They run under the subscriber's own actor -- the automation resolves
//      the owner from a subscription row it read first, and then writes as that
//      user. The engine borrows the customer's authority rather than out-ranking
//      it, exactly as component/campaigns does for a send, and for the same
//      reason: no path in this domain should be able to touch a row its nominal
//      owner could not.
//
//   3. THE LIFECYCLE AUTOMATIONS, through the provisioning actions.
//      `createInstance` and the `*InstanceStatus` family. Same actor-borrowing
//      rule.
//
// WHY THE STATUS TRANSITIONS ARE SEPARATE MUTATIONS rather than one
// `setInstanceStatus(status:)`. A single parameterised setter would make every
// transition legal from every state and would put the state machine in the
// caller's hands -- including a caller replaying a webhook. Named transitions
// keep the machine in the DSL where it can be read, and make an illegal move
// (resuming something that was torn down) a mutation that does not exist rather
// than an argument nobody validated.

use fleet.concepts.{ instance, subscriber, subscription, usageMeter }

// =============================================================================
//   subscriber -- the account
// =============================================================================

/// Create the caller's MemQL Cloud account. Called at signup, BEFORE checkout and before any
/// instance exists, so a checkout that is abandoned leaves an account with no subscription rather
/// than a half-written record. Owned: ownerUserId is stamped from actor.userId, so a caller can
/// only ever create their own account.
@actor
mutate subscriber createSubscriber {
  args {
    subscriberId  string!
    companyName   string!
    billingEmail  string!
  }
  insert {
    accept { companyName, billingEmail }
    stamp {
      id: args.subscriberId
      ownerUserId: actor.userId
      status: "active"
    }
  }
}

/// Update the account's billing details. Owned -- ownerUserId is re-stamped from actor.userId, and
/// the engine's row-authz write guard refuses the update outright when the target row belongs to
/// somebody else.
@actor
mutate subscriber updateSubscriberBilling {
  args {
    subscriberId  string!
    companyName   string
    billingEmail  string
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriberId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    companyName: args.companyName
    billingEmail: args.billingEmail
  }
}

/// Record the Stripe customer id on an account, once, after the first checkout session completes.
/// Written from the webhook path. Not customer-callable in any meaningful sense: a caller who
/// pointed their account at another customer's `cus_...` would be re-parenting somebody else's
/// billing history, and the only thing standing between that and reality is that this value comes
/// out of a signature-verified event body rather than out of a form.
@actor
mutate subscriber linkStripeCustomer {
  args {
    subscriberId      string!
    stripeCustomerId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriberId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    stripeCustomerId: args.stripeCustomerId
  }
}

/// Move an account into or out of delinquency. Written by the dunning automations. Delinquency does
/// NOT stop service on its own -- see the concept's status doc for why cutting a customer off over
/// an expired card loses both the customer and the invoice.
@actor
mutate subscriber setSubscriberStatus {
  args {
    subscriberId  string!
    status        string!  @enum("active", "delinquent", "cancelled")
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriberId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: args.status
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   subscription -- the plan
// =============================================================================

/// Create a subscription for the caller's account. Called from the checkout-completed webhook path
/// with the tier Stripe actually charged for -- not from the signup form, which only expresses what
/// the customer selected. The difference matters: a subscription created from an intent rather than
/// from a payment is a subscription that can exist without money behind it.
@actor
mutate subscription createSubscription {
  args {
    subscriptionId  string!
    subscriberId    string!
    tier            string!  @enum("trial", "node", "graph", "mesh", "enterprise")
    billingPeriod   string   @enum("monthly", "annual")
    status          string!  @enum("trialing", "active", "past_due", "paused", "cancelled")
    trialEndsAt     datetime
    billingEmail    string!
  }
  insert {
    accept { subscriberId, tier, status, trialEndsAt, billingEmail }
    stamp {
      id: args.subscriptionId
      ownerUserId: actor.userId
      billingPeriod: args.billingPeriod ?? "monthly"
      haAddOn: false
    }
  }
}

/// Record the Stripe subscription and price ids. Written from the webhook path; this is the join
/// key every later Stripe event resolves against.
@actor
mutate subscription linkStripeSubscription {
  args {
    subscriptionId        string!
    stripeSubscriptionId  string!
    stripePriceId         string
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    stripeSubscriptionId: args.stripeSubscriptionId
    stripePriceId: args.stripePriceId
  }
}

/// Move a subscription to 'active' -- payment succeeded and service is owed. Also clears the grace
/// clock, because a subscription that is active is not in any grace period by definition, and
/// leaving a stale graceEndsAt behind is how a recovered account gets torn down a week later.
@actor
mutate subscription activateSubscription {
  args {
    subscriptionId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "active"
    graceEndsAt: ""
  }
}

/// Mirror the current billing period from Stripe. Its passing is what rolls the usage meters and
/// resets allowances, so this is written on every `invoice.paid` rather than computed locally --
/// month lengths, time zones and proration are Stripe's problem and getting them subtly wrong here
/// would show up as an allowance that resets on the wrong day.
@actor
mutate subscription setSubscriptionPeriod {
  args {
    subscriptionId      string!
    currentPeriodStart  datetime!
    currentPeriodEnd    datetime!
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    currentPeriodStart: args.currentPeriodStart
    currentPeriodEnd: args.currentPeriodEnd
  }
}

/// Move a subscription to 'past_due' and start the dunning clock. Service continues until
/// graceEndsAt passes; see the concept doc.
@actor
mutate subscription recordPaymentFailure {
  args {
    subscriptionId  string!
    graceEndsAt     datetime!
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "past_due"
    graceEndsAt: args.graceEndsAt
  }
}

/// Change the tier a subscription is on. The write the tier-change automation reacts to by
/// re-rendering the tenant's values. Customer-callable: an upgrade is a customer decision, and the
/// money follows through Stripe rather than being asserted here.
@actor
mutate subscription requestTierChange {
  args {
    subscriptionId  string!
    tier            string!  @enum("trial", "node", "graph", "mesh", "enterprise")
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    tier: args.tier
  }
}

/// Turn the paid add-ons on or off. HA is the only one: it is meaningful on Node, where HA is not in
/// the base price, and from Graph up the tier's own haIncluded wins and haAddOn is ignored. The
/// voice add-on went with the conversational product (epic memql#4988).
///
/// The name stays PLURAL. "The add-ons a subscription carries" is the category, not a count, so it
/// would have to be bought back the next time one lands. It is also not free to change: this bundle
/// is mounted at MEMQL_DSL_PATH rather than embedded, so sdk-gen never sees it and neither SDK
/// carries a generated method -- which means the callers that name it do so over the wire, from
/// Orbit, whose code is not in this repository. A rename here is a wire change whose blast radius
/// cannot be measured from inside this tree.
@actor
mutate subscription setSubscriptionAddOns {
  args {
    subscriptionId  string!
    haAddOn         bool!
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    haAddOn: args.haAddOn
  }
}

/// Schedule a cancellation. NOT immediate: the customer paid through the period, so the row stays
/// active until cancelAt passes. What this write does is set the date the lifecycle sweep acts on.
@actor
mutate subscription requestCancellation {
  args {
    subscriptionId  string!
    cancelAt        datetime!
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    cancelAt: args.cancelAt
  }
}

/// End a subscription: no further billing, and the grace clock on the data starts. Written by the
/// lifecycle sweep when cancelAt passes, and by the `customer.subscription.deleted` webhook.
@actor
mutate subscription endSubscription {
  args {
    subscriptionId  string!
    status          string!  @enum("paused", "cancelled")
    graceEndsAt     datetime!
  }
  update {
    id: args.subscriptionId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: args.status
    graceEndsAt: args.graceEndsAt
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   instance -- what runs
// =============================================================================

/// Record a tenant instance and start provisioning it. Written BEFORE any cluster object exists, so
/// that a provisioning run which dies halfway leaves a row saying what was being attempted rather
/// than an orphaned namespace nothing points at.
///
/// STAMPS `provisioning`, NOT `pending`, and the difference is the trigger. The lifecycle
/// automations treat the transient statuses as COMMANDS (see automations.memql), so a row created
/// as `pending` would sit there settled and inert -- correct-looking, and never provisioned.
/// `pending` remains in the enum for a row written ahead of any intent to act on it; creating one
/// is not how a customer's instance comes into being.
///
/// THE SHAPE ARGUMENTS ARE RESOLVED FROM THE TIER, NOT CHOSEN. A caller supplies instanceProfile,
/// dbPreset and haEnabled, and a caller could therefore ask for `dedicated` on a Node subscription.
/// What stops that is not this mutation -- it is that the caller is either the checkout automation
/// (which reads them off the tier's own spec row) or Orbit (which does the same), and that a wrong
/// shape is corrected on the next re-render from the same source. Recorded here because the
/// argument list looks like a choice and is not one.
@actor
mutate instance createInstance {
  args {
    instanceId       string!
    subscriptionId   string!
    subscriberId     string!
    name             string!
    domain           string!
    tier             string!  @enum("trial", "node", "graph", "mesh", "enterprise")
    instanceProfile  string!  @enum("solo", "standard", "dedicated")
    dbPreset         string!  @enum("entry", "mid", "top")
    haEnabled        bool!
    notifyEmail      string!
    maxLlmCalls      string!
    maxLlmCostUsd    string!
    trialEndsAt      datetime
  }
  insert {
    accept { subscriptionId, subscriberId, name, domain, tier, instanceProfile, dbPreset, haEnabled, notifyEmail, maxLlmCalls, maxLlmCostUsd, trialEndsAt }
    stamp {
      id: args.instanceId
      ownerUserId: actor.userId
      status: "provisioning"
      argoApplication: args.name
      namespace: args.name
    }
  }
}

/// Mark an instance as being brought up. The transient state Orbit renders as progress.
@actor
mutate instance markInstanceProvisioning {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "provisioning"
    lastError: ""
  }
}

/// Mark an instance as up and serving. Stamps provisionedAt, which is the customer-visible created
/// date and the start of any uptime commitment.
@actor
mutate instance markInstanceRunning {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "running"
    provisionedAt: now
    suspendedAt: ""
    lastError: ""
  }
}

/// Mark an instance as scaled to zero with its data intact. Stamps suspendedAt so the teardown and
/// hibernation sweeps can answer "how long has this been down" from one field read rather than by
/// walking row history.
@actor
mutate instance markInstanceSuspended {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "suspended"
    suspendedAt: now
  }
}

/// Mark an instance as torn down. Terminal: the volumes are gone and a resume is no longer possible,
/// which is why this is a different state from 'suspended' rather than a flag on it.
@actor
mutate instance markInstanceTornDown {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "torn_down"
  }
}

/// Record a failed lifecycle transition, verbatim from the capability script's JSON error envelope.
/// The row stays 'failed' until something retries it -- the control plane does not guess at whether
/// a half-provisioned tenant should be resumed or destroyed.
@actor
mutate instance markInstanceFailed {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
    lastError   string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "failed"
    lastError: args.lastError
  }
}

/// Re-stamp what an instance is actually rendered at, after a tier change reconciles. Separate from
/// the subscription's tier on purpose: this says what is RUNNING, which can lag what was BOUGHT by
/// one reconciliation, and conflating the two is how a tier change reports success before the pods
/// have rolled.
@actor
mutate instance setInstanceShape {
  args {
    instanceId       string!
    tier             string!  @enum("trial", "node", "graph", "mesh", "enterprise")
    instanceProfile  string!  @enum("solo", "standard", "dedicated")
    dbPreset         string!  @enum("entry", "mid", "top")
    haEnabled        bool!
    maxLlmCalls      string!
    maxLlmCostUsd    string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    tier: args.tier
    instanceProfile: args.instanceProfile
    dbPreset: args.dbPreset
    haEnabled: args.haEnabled
    maxLlmCalls: args.maxLlmCalls
    maxLlmCostUsd: args.maxLlmCostUsd
  }
}

/// Stamp the last time this instance served a request. The input to idle hibernation. Written by
/// the tenant's own heartbeat, because the control plane cannot see the tenant's traffic.
@actor
mutate instance recordInstanceActivity {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    lastActivityAt: now
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   usageMeter -- what is consumed
// =============================================================================

/// Open a meter for one (subscription, period, metric). The id is derived from those three values
/// by the caller, so opening the same period twice lands on the same row rather than opening a
/// second accumulator -- which is what makes metering idempotent under replay.
///
/// `included` is copied from the tier here, at period open, rather than looked up at check time.
/// See the concept header: an allowance is a promise about a period, and a period already in flight
/// must not be re-priced by a tier change or a price change.
@actor
mutate usageMeter openUsageMeter {
  args {
    meterId         string!
    subscriptionId  string!
    metric          string!  @enum("message_credits")
    periodStart     datetime!
    periodEnd       datetime!
    included        float!
  }
  insert {
    accept { subscriptionId, metric, periodStart, periodEnd, included }
    stamp {
      id: args.meterId
      ownerUserId: actor.userId
      consumed: 0
      overage: 0
      reportedToStripe: 0
      status: "open"
    }
  }
}

/// Write the accumulated totals for a meter. The caller supplies the new absolute values rather
/// than a delta, because the read-modify-write happens where the allowance decision happens -- the
/// same place that has to answer "does this turn get admitted", and splitting the two would let a
/// turn be admitted against a total that was already stale.
@actor
mutate usageMeter recordUsage {
  args {
    meterId   string!
    consumed  float!
    overage   float!
  }
  update {
    id: args.meterId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    consumed: args.consumed
    overage: args.overage
  }
}

/// Record how much of the overage has been pushed to Stripe. The difference between this and
/// `overage` is what the next push owes -- which is what makes the push idempotent under retry and
/// self-healing after a missed one, rather than a permanently lost charge.
@actor
mutate usageMeter markUsageReported {
  args {
    meterId           string!
    reportedToStripe  float!
  }
  update {
    id: args.meterId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    reportedToStripe: args.reportedToStripe
  }
}

/// Close a meter at period end. No further increments are accepted; a late one is then visibly
/// refused rather than landing silently on a period that has already been invoiced.
@actor
mutate usageMeter closeUsageMeter {
  args {
    meterId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.meterId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "closed"
  }
}

/// Mark a closed meter as fully reported -- its final overage reached Stripe and the row is history.
@actor
mutate usageMeter markUsageMeterReported {
  args {
    meterId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.meterId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "reported"
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   tierSpec -- the price list has NO mutation, deliberately
// =============================================================================
//
// There is no `publishTierSpec` here, and the omission is the design.
//
// The obvious shape -- an owner/admin-gated mutation that writes a tier row --
// cannot actually BE owner/admin-gated in this domain. A mutation carries no
// filter, so the DSL has no place to hang a caller predicate; the constructs
// that are gated that way in the engine tree (createDeployment,
// updateDeploymentStatus) are gated in GO, by deploycontrol.authorize, and
// their `requiresOwnerOrAdmin` spec is documentation of that Go gate rather
// than the gate itself. A bundle has no Go, so the same shape here would be a
// mutation any authenticated caller could reach, on a @rowAuthz(public)
// concept, with a doc comment claiming otherwise.
//
// That is not a theoretical hole. `included` on a usage meter is copied from
// the tier at period open; `instanceProfile` and `dbPreset` decide what
// infrastructure a purchase provisions. A caller who can write a tier row can
// mint themselves an allowance -- which is precisely the unbounded-spend path
// this epic's launch gate exists to forbid, arriving through the front door.
//
// So the catalog is SEEDED (seeds.memql) and a price change is a bundle change:
// edit the seed, review it, deploy it. That is not a workaround, it is the
// right amount of friction. A price is a promise to every subscription that
// names the tier, and "somebody could change it from a console at 2am" is not a
// property a price list should have. The GitOps path already gives us review,
// an audit trail, and a rollback.
//
// A price RISE publishes a NEW tier and marks the old one 'grandfathered'
// rather than editing the row in place: existing subscriptions name a tier by
// value, so editing the row they name re-prices them retroactively and
// silently.

// =============================================================================
//   Lifecycle COMMANDS -- the writes that make the controller act
// =============================================================================
//
// The three below write a TRANSIENT status, which the automations in
// automations.memql treat as a command (see that file's header). Nothing else in
// this domain writes one, and that is deliberate: the settled statuses are
// stamped by the controller when it finishes, so a caller who could write
// `running` directly could tell the fleet a tenant is up that was never
// provisioned.
//
// They are ordinary owned-tier writes because the customer legitimately asks for
// two of them (pause, cancel) from Orbit, and the third (teardown) is asked for
// by a sweep running under the same borrowed authority. What bounds them is not
// who calls them but that each is ONE named transition -- there is no
// `setInstanceStatus(status:)` a caller could point anywhere.

/// Ask the fleet to scale this instance to zero, leaving its data intact. Written by Orbit when a
/// customer pauses, by the trial-expiry sweep, and by the hibernation sweep. The controller picks it
/// up from the transient status and settles the row at `suspended`.
@actor
mutate instance requestInstanceSuspend {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "suspending"
  }
}

/// Ask the fleet to bring a suspended instance back up. Written by Orbit on resume and by the
/// trial-conversion path.
@actor
mutate instance requestInstanceResume {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "resuming"
  }
}

/// Ask the fleet to destroy this instance. IRREVERSIBLE once the controller acts on it -- the
/// capability script takes a final backup and then deletes the tenant's ArgoCD Application, which
/// cascades to its volumes.
///
/// Written only by the grace-expiry sweep, and never by Orbit. A customer cancelling schedules a
/// cancellation; what destroys their data is the grace period running out, days later, after the
/// export offer. A one-click "delete everything" in a console is a support ticket waiting to happen
/// and there is no reason to build one.
@actor
mutate instance requestInstanceTeardown {
  args {
    instanceId  string!
  }
  update {
    id: args.instanceId
    ownerUserId: actor.userId
    status: "tearing_down"
  }
}
